# Rigor core overlay — supplemental core signatures.
#
# --- Integer#bit_count (Ruby 4.1) ---
#
# Issue #1691. Ruby 4.1 adds `Integer#bit_count`, the population count of
# the receiver's binary representation (`numeric.c`, call-seq
# `bit_count -> integer`). No rbs release through 4.2 declares it, so
# `5.bit_count` reported `call.undefined-method` on correct 4.1 code.
#
# TEMPORARY: delete this file once the rbs release Rigor bundles declares
# the method upstream.
#
# Declared unconditionally, not gated on `target_ruby`: a 4.0-targeted
# project calling it is an error Rigor then misses, but gating would report
# every correct 4.1 call under the default target (ADR-121 § Rejected
# alternatives; false positives outrank).
#
# Declared on a module `Integer` includes, not as a direct `def`: a second
# direct `def` from a later rbs or a project `sig/` is a
# `DuplicatedMethodDefinitionError` that takes all of `Integer` down to
# `Dynamic[top]`, while a direct `def` from either source simply overrides
# a method the class gets through `include` (see `process.rbs`).
module RigorCoreOverlay
  module Ruby41Integer
    def bit_count: () -> Integer
  end
end

class Integer
  include RigorCoreOverlay::Ruby41Integer
end
